{"name":"Blue Hue","hex":"#394d60","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"hue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A color, or shade of color; tint; dye.","The characteristic related to the light frequency that appears in the color, for instance red, yellow, green, cyan, blue or magenta."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shout or cry."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hue","blocks":[{"text":"In color theory, hue is one of the properties of a color, defined in the CIECAM02 model as \"the degree to which a stimulus can be described as similar to or different from stimuli that are described as red, orange, yellow, green, blue, violet\", within certain theories of color vision."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Colors-i54-ring.png/330px-Colors-i54-ring.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hue","wikidata":"Q372948"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Munsell_hue_wheel_equal_value_and_chroma.png/500px-Munsell_hue_wheel_equal_value_and_chroma.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Munsell_hue_wheel_equal_value_and_chroma.png/1280px-Munsell_hue_wheel_equal_value_and_chroma.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Munsell_hue_wheel_equal_value_and_chroma.png/500px-Munsell_hue_wheel_equal_value_and_chroma.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gradient Munsell hue wheel at value 5 and constant chroma (6.24)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMunsell_hue_wheel_equal_value_and_chroma.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/HSV_cone.jpg/500px-HSV_cone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/HSV_cone.jpg/500px-HSV_cone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"HSV color space as a conical object","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHSV_cone.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Hue_shift_six_photoshop.jpg/250px-Hue_shift_six_photoshop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Hue_shift_six_photoshop.jpg/330px-Hue_shift_